    "final.course_reflection": "What stood out to me about this activity was being able to watch the robot move based on the code that I wrote. It was definitely rewarding to see it move after working on the code and troubleshooting issues that I faced with the setup of my virtual environment. This brings me to my next point, that this lab reminded me that robotics can be frustrating, as sometimes the code itself is fine, but something in the environment is not working properly. I think it will get easier as I get more used to navigating through it, as for now I still feel slightly afraid of it.  Overall, I feel excited for what's next and I can't wait to start working on the group project. I can only imagine how cool it would be to see Misty move based on the code my group and I will come up with.",
    "final.synthesis": "A robot motion program can be technically correct and still fail as a part of a sociotechnical system for various reasons. For example, a robot could follow the expected path through a hallway, but it might get in the way of people walking there or someone using a wheelchair. People may also not know where the robot is going to move next which could make them feel uncomfortable or unsafe. Testing the robot for accuracy is important, but it doesn't guarantee that it will work well in a shared space. To evaluate this issue, I would want to see how the robot does in a hallway with people in it and see how people are able to coexist with the robot. I would be interested in how easily people can move around it and how the robot responds when someone gets in its way.",
